|  The expert continued to pontificate about the mother’s condition of
|  enmeshment with her child, spinning a broader, long-term view:Um, we’re supposed to give our children roots and wings and in
|  enmeshment you’re kind of overvaluing the roots and not paying
|  attention enough to the wings. Children need to grow away from
|  us eventually, not at seven, but you know, eventually.\123
|  
|  So, whether it’s PAS that is called outright or its derivative, referred
|  to as enmeshment, the implications are still the same. The child is,
|  according to these quack theories permeating the family courts, endangered by the mother’s caring and protective response to her child when
|  learning of the sexual violations of the child by the father. Unfortunately, in the mad world of family courts the solution is often nothing
